(EXP-74: a radix word may be inserted right before `number` to parse text in a
 base other than 10.
 Also covers: EXP-75 plain `as a number` is base 10, EXP-76 `as a hex number`
 and `as a hexadecimal number` are base 16, EXP-77 `as an octal number` is
 base 8, EXP-78 `as a binary number` is base 2, EXP-79 the spaced
 `as a base N number` form, EXP-80 the fused `as a baseN number` form.
 Every example value is the manual's own, so a mismatch here is a mismatch
 with the table rather than with my arithmetic.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox EXP-74.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   42
   255
   255
   15
   10
   45694
   2334
   2334
)
Print "42" as a number.
Print "ff" as a hex number.
Print "ff" as a hexadecimal number.
Print "17" as an octal number.
Print "1010" as a binary number.
Print "z9a" as a base 36 number.
